{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_fpvmy7hner55yjag3ivgkmzzdyzg5yvwovqbcy7b2b2ji44yt42a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The library implementation is complete and compiles correctly. Here's a summary:\n\n## Implementation\n\nI implemented the two functions in `src/morton.rs` as direct safe-Rust ports of the C code in `c_src/morton.c`:\n\n**`morton(hi, lo)`** — Interleaves bits of two 32-bit values into a single 64-bit value, using the bit-spreading technique (the non-BMI2 fallback path from the C source). Each value is spread out so its bits occupy alternating positions, then `lo` fills the even bits and `hi << 1` fills the odd bits.\n\n**`unmortoner(x)`** — Compacts the even-positioned bits of `x` into a 32-bit value (the C `unmortoner` static helper). The C `unmorton` function uses this to recover both `hi` and `lo` (calling it on `z` and `z >> 1`).\n\nI converted C octal literals (`020`, `010`, `004`, `002`, `001`) to decimal (`16`, `8`, `4`, `2`, `1`).\n\n## Build/Test Status\n\n- `cargo build --lib` ✅ compiles cleanly\n- `cargo test --lib` ✅ passes (0 lib tests defined)\n- `cargo test` ❌ fails to compile — but **not** because of my code. The test file `src/bin/test.rs` has a pre-existing bug: at lines 32–33 it references a variable `z` that is never defined (the C version has `unsigned long long z = morton(x, y);` but the Rust port omitted that line). The rules forbid modifying anything under `src/bin/`, so this cannot be fixed from my side.\n\nThe library code itself is a faithful, safe (no `unsafe`, no FFI) translation of the C reference and would pass the test once the missing `let z = morton(x, y);` line is restored."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":238,"cache_read_input_tokens":22956,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":238,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":36,"service_tier":